    "content": "# Indonesia EV Charging Guide 2026 — Jakarta to Bali & Beyond\n\nIndonesia's EV transition is gathering real momentum. With the government targeting 15 million electric two-wheelers and 2 million electric cars by 2030, the charging infrastructure is expanding rapidly — especially along the Java toll road corridor connecting Jakarta, Bandung, Semarang, Surabaya, and Bali.\n\nThis is the most comprehensive guide to EV charging in Indonesia in 2026.\n\n## The EV Charging Landscape in Indonesia (2026)\n\nIndonesia's charging network (known as **SPKLU** — Stasiun Pengisian Kendaraan Listrik Umum) has grown substantially:\n\n- **2,100+** public charging stations\n- **5,800+** charging points\n- **PLN** (state electricity company) operates the largest network\n- **CCS2** is the official standard, with some CHAdeMO legacy units\n- Key private players: **PLN Icon+**, **Charge+**, **V-GREEN**\n\n### Major Charging Networks\n\n| Network | Stations | Connectors | Payment |\n|---------|----------|------------|---------|\n| **PLN Icon+** | 900+ | CCS2, Type 2 AC | App / QR |\n| **Charge+** | 450+ | CCS2 | App / QR |\n| **V-GREEN** (Hyundai) | 300+ | CCS2, CHAdeMO | App / Card |\n| **GESITS** | 200+ | CCS2 | App / QR |\n| **E-Mobility** (Toyota) | 150+ | CCS2 | App |\n\n## Charging Along the Java Toll Road\n\nThe **Trans-Java Toll Road** (Jakarta → Surabaya, ~760 km) is now fully EV-drivable.\n\n### Jakarta → Bandung (~150 km)\n- **Puncak corridor**: Multiple PLN stations every 30 km\n- **Cipularang Toll**: New 120 kW chargers at rest areas\n- **Bandung city**: 40+ stations, concentrated in Dago and the city center\n\n### Bandung → Semarang (~350 km)\n- **Brebes Exit**: PLN 60 kW station at rest area KM 260\n- **Pemalang**: V-GREEN 120 kW — the fastest on this stretch\n- **Semarang**: 25+ stations including malls and hotels\n\n### Semarang → Surabaya (~320 km)\n- **Ngawi**: PLN 60 kW (new, opened March 2026)\n- **Madiun**: Charge+ 100 kW\n- **Surabaya**: 50+ stations, Tunjungan Plaza has DC fast charging\n\n### Bali (Tourist Hub)\nBali has **80+ charging stations**, mostly concentrated in South Bali (Kuta, Seminyak, Canggu, Sanur), Ubud, Nusa Dua, and Denpasar.\n\n## Best EV Charging Apps for Indonesia\n\n### 1. PLN Mobile\nEssential but mixed reviews. Always call the station contact before relying on a specific charger.\n\n### 2. Charge+\nMore reliable real-time data. Good coverage in Jakarta, Bandung, and Surabaya.\n\n### 3. PlugShare Indonesia\nCommunity check-ins invaluable for verifying working stations.\n\n## Charging Costs in Indonesia\n\n- **PLN DC fast charging**: Rp 2,467/kWh (~$0.15 USD)\n- **PLN AC charging**: Rp 1,644/kWh (~$0.10 USD)\n- **Private chargers**: Rp 3,500-5,000/kWh (~$0.22-0.31 USD)\n- **Hotel charging**: Often free for guests\n\n**Real-world cost**: A full charge for a BYD Dolphin costs roughly **Rp 111,000 ($7 USD)** at a PLN DC charger.\n\n## Top EV Road Trip Routes in Indonesia\n\n### 1. Jakarta → Bandung → Semarang → Surabaya (Full Java Crossing)\n**Distance**: ~760 km\n**Charging stops**: 4-5 stops (PLN Icon+ stations every 60-80 km)\n\n### 2. Denpasar → Ubud → Bedugul → Singaraja (Bali Loop)\n**Distance**: ~200 km\n**Tip**: The climb to Bedugul uses more battery, but regen descent recovers some.\n\n### 3. Surabaya → Malang → Batu\n**Distance**: ~90 km\n**Best for**: Mountain scenery and Gunung Bromo approach.\n\n## Practical Tips\n\n- **Payment**: GoPay, OVO, QRIS — all widely accepted\n- **Flood season** (Nov-Mar): Lower ground stations may be inaccessible\n- **Hotel charging**: Increasingly common at mid-range and luxury hotels (Aryaduta, Accor, JW Marriott)\n\n## The Future (2026-2027)\n\nPLN targeting 5,000 stations by 2027, battery swapping for two-wheelers, and the new capital (IKN Nusantara) designed as EV-first city.\n\n<!-- affiliate:booking.com/search?destination=Indonesia&filter=ev_charging -->",
